  alone.

  ---

  [1;3;4mDesign — Section 1 of 3: Architecture and module layout[0m

  Four files under [38;5;153msrc/logger/[39m, each ending with a small dual-export footer so the same file loads via [38;5;153mrequire[39m in Node
  and via [38;5;153m<script>[39m in the browser:

  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/logger/redact.js[0m — the security boundary, isolated on purpose. Exports [38;5;153mALLOWED_CTX_FIELDS[39m and a pure [38;5;153mredact(ctx)
[39m    that returns a new object containing only allowlisted keys. Depends on nothing.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/logger/core.js[0m — [38;5;153mcreateLogger({ level, transports, runtime })[39m returning [38;5;153m{ debug, info, warn, error }[39m. Pure
    record assembly and level filtering. Touches no [38;5;153mwindow[39m, no [38;5;153mprocess[39m, no network. Every transport call is wrapped so a
    broken transport cannot propagate an exception into your app.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/logger/browser.js[0m — console transport, batching HTTP transport, [38;5;153mwindow[39m error handlers, session ID. Exposes
    [38;5;153minitLogger(config)[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/logger/node.js[0m — stdout/stderr transport, [38;5;153mprocess[39m error handlers, env-var config. Exposes [38;5;153minitLogger()[39m.

  The dependency direction is one-way: adapters depend on core, core depends on redact, redact depends on nothing.
  That's what makes the allowlist testable without a DOM, a network, or a running app.

  Two notes on what I am deliberately [3mnot[0m doing. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains three [38;5;153m<script>[39m tags before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m rather than any
  build step. And [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m's [38;5;153mconsole.log(greet('world'))[39m stays a [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m — that line is the program's [3moutput[0m,
  not a diagnostic, and routing it through the logger would send "Hello, world!" to your production log endpoint.

  [1mTooling.[0m This repo has no configured tooling at all: no [38;5;153mscripts[39m, no linter, no test directory, no dependencies.
  Setting tooling up is cheapest now, before the code exists, and whatever you pick lands in the spec's constraints so
  every later task inherits it. Relevant specifics here: Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m and [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m would give you unit
  tests with [3mzero[0m added dependencies, matching the repo's current character; ESLint + Prettier are dev-only
  dependencies; end-to-end testing would mean Playwright and a real browser, which is heavy for a login stub; and
  property-based testing (fast-check) is unusually well-suited to a redactor, since "no non-allowlisted key ever
  survives" is exactly the kind of invariant you can throw generated garbage at.

